The latency-associated transcript (LAT) in Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) serves as a mechanism to maintain viral latency by regulating the expression of both lysogenic and lytic genes. LAT inhibits the transcription of lytic genes, such as the viral structural proteins (gp3, gp4, and(gp60)), thereby preventing viral replication while allowing the maintenance of the viral episomal genome. This selective regulation promotes viral persistence without inducing cell death.

