A mechanical vector in the context of poxvirus transmission refers to an organism that spreads the virus without becoming infected itself. This allows the virus to be transmitted from one host to another indirectly. For example, fleas or insects may serve as vectors by biting hosts, carrying the virus to new hosts upon feeding. This mechanism enables the spread of the virus within ecosystems where direct contact between hosts might not occur frequently.

Answer choice A describes genetic engineering methods used in research, which is unrelated to the natural mode of transmission via vectors. Answer choice B accurately describes how mechanical vectors function during disease spread.
